[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  thursday september 1st i'm zoe thomas  for the wall street journal  a first of its kind bill passed  unanimously in california this week  it would require social media platforms  like facebook snapchat and tick tock to  consider the physical and mental health  of minors when designing their products  after the bill passed supporters spoke  outside the state house about its  potential impact  among them was 18 year old emmy kim from  logoff a teen-led organization  supporting the bill  kim said she faced online bullying as a  teenager  safety parameters exist on everything  that kids touch except social media  social media was not designed for  children it was designed for adults  it's now time you design it for us  the bill now heads to the desk of  california governor gavin newsom he  hasn't said if he'll sign or veto it and  a spokesperson declined to comment  but how much of an impact can this one  state level law have  and is it a sign of more regulation on  social media to come  joining us to discuss this is our tech  reporter megan bobarowski hi megan  thanks for coming back on the show hi  zoe  so let's start with what exactly is in  this bill  sure so this bill would make it so that  social media companies going forward  would have to keep the well-being of  children in mind when they're designing  products and features that children are  likely going to access  children being defined as anyone under  the age of 18. and so how would social  media companies be expected to do that  and be held accountable for doing that  so the social media companies would have  to do a risk assessment basically  assessing whether their products do harm  children and then they'd have to submit  that report to the government and if the  government at any point finds that they  are in violation of this rule you know  they do have features that are you know  harmful to children they have 90 days to  fix the problem to you know address the  feature or  shift the algorithm and if they don't  complete that in 90 days then they are  subject to fines of up to 7 500 a child  which you know if you have potentially  hundreds of thousands of children on  these apps that's a lot of money yeah i  can see that working out to be a  potentially pretty hefty fine one thing  when i uh kind of glanced through what  was in the bill that stood out to me was  this requirement that their privacy  policies be in language that children  can understand i mean how did they  determine that and are we going to  expect children to read these privacy  rules any more than we expect adults to  so a lot of this i think is not clearly  spelled out as in this is the language  you need and here's what you're gonna do  but there's very similar laws that have  been enacted in europe in the uk and so  this bill is actually very much based on  uk law and so you can sort of look to  that to see the types of things that  might be targeted here so for instance  in the uk google has to make safe search  which screens out any sort of  inappropriate content they have to make  that their default setting for children  and tick tock and instagram  have disabled direct messaging between  children and adults they do not follow  on the platform so it's things like this  that you can sort of look to as examples  of ways that the companies might have to  tweak or alter the way they operate and  it's actually the way they do operate  abroad  how are the social media companies  reacting to this bill passing so  representatives from  meta snap twitter have lobbied against  the spill they did not want it to pass  there's a group called technet that  lobbies on behalf of these tech groups  and they oppose the bill and they say  that you know because it's at the state  level this is going to create problems  for them in terms of you know they don't  operate at a state level they operate  nationally and internationally and so  having what they call a patchwork of  different laws in different states will  make it really hard for them to regulate  and do what they need to do but you know  this law  has already been basically enacted in  the uk and so they actually are already  doing some of this  abroad can you just take us briefly  through how we got to this point i mean  this wasn't the first bill of its kind  to try and pass in california  yeah so there have been a slew of social  media bills that have been  going through the legislature there was  one that died last month that would have  allowed  state attorneys general to sue the  companies if they believed they were  harming children so the social media  companies  preferred this one that has passed it's  a bit more lenient you know it's not  like anyone's going to be suing them  over this  they still oppose it but they previously  said you know that they wanted  this bill over potentially any other  bills that would allow either the state  or parents even there's at one point  there was a bill that would have allowed  parents to sue the social media  companies they obviously were not in  favor of that okay so this is a law on  the state level people have called for  more federal action we've certainly been  hearing about the impact of social media  for a couple of years do we expect other  states to follow this precedence up by  california or for the federal government  to step in and do anything  certainly you have some people who are  saying this needs to happen on the  federal level and are advocating for  that but you know california is really  the leader here and the proponents of  the bill say if we can pass this here  sometimes california does it first and  the rest of the country follows and so i  think the sort of thought process for  the proponents of this bill is if we  pass it here then other states will see  this and pass their own versions of that  whereas the opponents say no let's do  one at the federal level which might be  more difficult to pass but actually in  california this bill had bipartisan  support so actually maybe not  so what comes next i mean when can we  expect to possibly see changes to these  platforms so the bill still needs to be  signed by governor newsome and he has  not said whether or not he will sign the  bill  but if the governor signs the bill and  it goes into law these provisions  requiring social media companies  to design their platforms with children  in mind will go into effect in july 2024  and so  any features or platforms or anything  created after that will be subject to  these laws but you know anything that  exists if they run the assessment and  say  this doesn't pass our tests you know  this isn't okay they would then have to  make those changes  all right that's our reporter megan  bobrowski thanks for joining us megan  thanks for having me and that's it for  today's tech news briefing  if you want more tech stories check out  our website  wsj.com and if you enjoy listening to  our show please leave us a five star  rating and a review on apple podcast  spotify or wherever you get your  podcasts  i'm zoe thomas for the wall street  journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]  you

